winner +9 and each opponent −3. If the strongest declared hands tie exactly, the non-winning players each pay the call value and the tied winners share that pool equally. For example, two tied three-point callers get +3 each, and the other two players lose 3 each. Equal shares can include fractions. If everyone ties, no points move.</li><li>Starting to the dealer’s right, a player holding the same rank can play their matching card plus another card from their hand. The turned-up card stays on the table.</li><li>The other card played becomes the next rank to match. Chances to use it pass to the right. You can decline.</li><li>With a pair and one different card (for example 2, 2, King), keep the pair and wait for a matching table card for the different rank to win. A card matching the pair is automatically skipped; this applies to every pair, for people and computers. Match the different card to win and clear all three cards, including the pair. Your chance must come in rotation, including after someone declines a drawn card.</li><li>With just one card left, use a matching table card to play your last card alone and win.</li><li>If nobody uses the opening card, the second player to the dealer’s right draws. A draw turns up one card; it does not enter anyone’s hand. The drawer has first choice, then chances pass to the right. If nobody uses it, the next player draws.</li><li>Only the strongest callers’ call names stay beside their seats. Each player’s played cards stay face up beside their seat until the hand ends, so everyone can verify the plays. Unplayed opponent cards stay face down beside those cards. The centre pile still shows only its latest card. Winning call cards that have not yet been played stay hidden. Only after the hand is complete are the strongest callers’ original cards revealed at their seats; tied strongest callers each show their cards. When a player wins the matching-card hand, their finishing cards are shown face up in front of them with VICTORY until the next game.</li><li>The matching-card hand winner receives 1 point from each opponent: +1 with 2 players, +2 with 3 players, or +3 with 4 players. Every opponent loses 1 point. These hand points are separate from calls and dealer bonuses. The winner deals next.</li><li>If the deck runs out, no hand points change, but all call points and the dealer’s bonus are kept. The game counts; everyone picks again to select a dealer.</li></ol><p>Agree on 5, 10, 15 games or more. At the end, positive scores win; negative scores lose; zero is even. Add more games in steps of 5 by agreement.</p><p>Two, three, or four players can play. For computer play, choose the total number of players: you plus one, two, or three computers. Online, the host chooses 2, 3, or 4 players and starts when that many are connected once everyone chooses the same game count and taps Ready. Online play pauses if someone disconnects. Reopen this page on the same browser to reconnect. Tables are currently held in server memory and can be lost when the server restarts.</p><button id="closeRules">Back to the table</button></dialog>
